SED(1)                           User Commands                          SED(1)

NAME
       sed - stream editor for filtering and transforming text

SYNOPSIS
       sed [OPTION]... {script} [input-file]...

DESCRIPTION
       sed reads its input a line at a time, applies the commands of the
       script to each line in turn, and writes the result to standard output.

       If no -e or -f option is given, the first non-option argument is the
       script.  With no input file, or with -, standard input is read.

HOW IT WORKS
       Understanding three things explains almost every sed script:

       o      The current line is held in the PATTERN SPACE.  Every command in
              the script is considered in order; a command whose address
              matches runs.  At the end of the script the pattern space is
              printed -- unless -n -- and the next line is read.

       o      A separate HOLD SPACE persists across lines and is the only
              memory a script has.  It starts empty.  g, G, h, H and x move
              text between the two.

       o      The trailing newline is NOT part of the pattern space.  That is
              why $ in a regular expression matches end of line rather than
              the newline itself, and why N and G insert one explicitly.

OPTIONS
       -n, --quiet, --silent
              suppress the automatic printing of the pattern space

       -e SCRIPT, --expression=SCRIPT
              add SCRIPT to the commands to be executed.  May be repeated;
              the scripts are joined with newlines.

       -f FILE, --file=FILE
              add the contents of FILE to the commands to be executed

       -i[SUFFIX], --in-place[=SUFFIX]
              edit files in place.  If SUFFIX is given, the original is kept
              under that suffix.  The suffix is attached to the option, never
              given as a separate argument: `sed -i.bak s/a/b/ f` keeps f.bak,
              whereas `sed -i s/a/b/ f` keeps nothing.  A * in SUFFIX is
              replaced by the file name, which is how a backup directory is
              requested.

       -E, -r, --regexp-extended
              use extended regular expressions

       -s, --separate
              treat the input files as separate streams rather than as one.
              Line numbering restarts and $ matches the last line of each
              file.  Implied by -i.

       -z, --null-data
              separate lines by NUL characters rather than newlines

       --posix
              disable the extensions.  Currently this affects N at end of
              input: POSIX discards the pattern space, the default prints it.

       --help display this help and exit

       --version
              output version information and exit

ADDRESSES
       A command with no address runs on every line.  With one address it
       runs on matching lines; with two, on the range between them.  A ! after
       the address inverts the selection.

       N      line number N

       $      the last line of the input

       /REGEXP/, \cREGEXPc
              lines matching REGEXP.  The second form uses c as the delimiter,
              which avoids escaping slashes in a path.  I after the closing
              delimiter matches case-insensitively, M makes ^ and $ match at
              embedded newlines.

       FIRST~STEP
              line FIRST and every STEP lines after it

       ADDR1,ADDR2
              from a line matching ADDR1 to the next line matching ADDR2

       ADDR1,+N
              ADDR1 and the N lines following it

       ADDR1,~N
              from ADDR1 to the next line whose number is a multiple of N

       0,/REGEXP/
              like 1,/REGEXP/ except that REGEXP may match on the FIRST line.
              This is the only way to select up to the first match inclusive.

       A range becomes active when ADDR1 matches and stays active until ADDR2
       matches on a LATER line -- ADDR2 is never tested on the line that
       opened the range.  So /a/,/a/ spans from one matching line to the next,
       not a single line.

COMMANDS
       {COMMANDS}
              run COMMANDS as a group

       =      print the current line number

       a TEXT append TEXT after the pattern space is printed

       b LABEL
              branch to LABEL, or to the end of the script if omitted

       c TEXT replace the selected lines with TEXT.  For a range the text
              appears once, at the end of the range.

       d      delete the pattern space and start the next cycle

       D      delete up to the first newline in the pattern space and restart
              the script WITHOUT reading a new line

       g      replace the pattern space with the hold space

       G      append a newline and the hold space to the pattern space

       h      replace the hold space with the pattern space

       H      append a newline and the pattern space to the hold space

       i TEXT insert TEXT before the pattern space

       l [N]  print the pattern space unambiguously, escaping non-printing
              characters and wrapping at N columns (default 70)

       n      print the pattern space (unless -n) and read the next line

       N      append a newline and the next line to the pattern space

       p      print the pattern space

       P      print the pattern space up to the first newline

       q [N]  print the pattern space, then exit with status N

       Q [N]  exit with status N without printing

       r FILE queue the contents of FILE to be output at the end of the cycle.
              A missing file is silently ignored.

       R FILE queue one line of FILE

       s/RE/REPL/FLAGS
              substitute; see SUBSTITUTION below

       t LABEL
              branch to LABEL if an s command has succeeded since the last
              input line was read or the last t

       T LABEL
              branch to LABEL if no s command has succeeded

       w FILE write the pattern space to FILE

       W FILE write the first line of the pattern space to FILE

       x      exchange the pattern and hold spaces

       y/SRC/DST/
              transliterate the characters of SRC to those of DST

       z      empty the pattern space

       F      print the name of the current input file

       :LABEL define a branch target

       #      comment to end of line

SUBSTITUTION
       s/REGEXP/REPLACEMENT/FLAGS replaces text matching REGEXP.  Any
       character may be used as the delimiter in place of /.

       In REPLACEMENT:

       &      the whole matched text

       \1 to \9
              the text matched by the corresponding \( \) group

       \n, \t, \r, \f, \v, \a
              the corresponding control character

       \U, \L turn the rest of the replacement upper or lower case, until \E

       \u, \l turn the next character upper or lower case

       \E     end a \U or \L

       Flags:

       g      replace every match, not just the first

       N      replace only the Nth match

       Ng     replace the Nth match and all after it

       p      print the pattern space if a substitution was made

       i, I   match case-insensitively

       m, M   make ^ and $ match at embedded newlines

       w FILE write the pattern space to FILE if a substitution was made

EXIT STATUS
       0      the script ran successfully

       1      the script asked to quit with a non-zero status

       2      an input file could not be read

       4      an I/O error occurred

EXAMPLES
       Replace the first occurrence on each line, and every occurrence:

              sed 's/old/new/'  file
              sed 's/old/new/g' file

       Print only matching lines, the grep of sed:

              sed -n '/pattern/p' file

       Delete a range:

              sed '2,4d' file

       Join every pair of lines.  \n is a real newline here -- that is the
       whole reason N is useful:

              sed 'N;s/\n/ /' file

       Reverse the file, the classic hold-space idiom:

              sed -n '1!G;h;$p' file

       Join the whole file into one comma-separated line, using a branch:

              sed -n ':a;$!{N;ba};s/\n/,/gp' file

       Edit in place, keeping a backup:

              sed -i.bak 's/old/new/g' file

       Print the first line only, which needs the 0 address form:

              sed -n '0,/./p' file

NOTES
       s///g always makes progress on an empty match: after matching nothing,
       the scan advances one character.  Without that a pattern such as x*
       would substitute forever.

       Character escapes are expanded before the regular expression is
       compiled, because POSIX regular expressions have no \n of their own --
       to the matcher, \n would otherwise be an escaped letter n.  Regex
       syntax (\( \) \1 \. \* \[ \\) and the word operators (\b \w \s \< \>)
       are left untouched.

SEE ALSO
       grep(1), expr(1), tr(1), cut(1)
